The '''Aston Martin Valkyrie''' (also known by its code-names as '''AM-RB 001''' and '''Nebula''') is a limited production [[Hybrid electric vehicle|hybrid]] [[sports car]] collaboratively built by British automobile manufacturers [[Aston Martin]], [[Red Bull Racing#Red Bull Advanced Technologies|Red Bull Racing Advanced Technologies]] and several other parties, in order to develop a track-oriented car entirely usable and enjoyable as a road car, conceived by [[Adrian Newey]], [[Andy Palmer]], [[Christian Horner]] and Simon Sproule;<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.astonmartin.com/en/live/news/2016/07/05/aston-martin-and-red-bull-racing-unveil-radical-am-rb-001-hypercar |title=Aston Martin and Red Bull Racing unveil radical AM-RB 001 hypercar |date=5 July 2016 |website=Aston Martin |access-date=24 August 2016}}</ref> Newey, who was Red Bull Racing's Chief Technical Officer at the time, contributed directly to the design of the car.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.sportsmole.co.uk/formula-1/red-bull/news/horner-newey-fully-motivated-again_278419.html |title=Christian Horner: 'Adrian Newey fully motivated again' |date=2016 |website=Sportsmole |access-date=24 August 2016}}</ref>

== Nomenclature ==
The original codename was '''Nebula''', an [[acronym]] for Newey, Red Bull and Aston Martin Lagonda. The name '''AM-RB 001''' was chosen as the final codename, and was decided since Aston Martin and Red Bull Racing had collaborated all throughout the project. '''AM''' stood for Aston Martin and '''RB''' stood for Red Bull. '''001''' may be a reference to it being the first production car the two have collaborated on.

In March 2017, Aston Martin revealed that the car would be named [[Valkyrie]], after the [[Norse mythology|Norse mythological]] figures.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.topgear.com/car-news/Geneva-motor-show/Aston-martin-names-its-hyper-car-Valkyrie |title=Aston Martin names its hypercar 'Valkyrie' |last=Rix |first=Jack |date=6 March 2017 |website=Top Gear |access-date=6 March 2017}}</ref> According to Red Bull, the name was chosen to continue the tradition of "V" nomenclature of Aston Martin's automobiles and to distinguish the vehicle as a high-performance car (the "V" was used as the distinguishing factor).<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.redbull.com/en/motorsports/stories/1331848795383/aston-martin-red-bull-racing-valkyrie-hypercar |title=Codename AM-RB 001 becomes Aston Martin Valkyrie |last=Schran |first=Oliver |date=14 March 2017 |website=Red Bull |access-date=16 March 2017 }}</ref> The [[Aston Martin Valhalla]], which is known as "Son of Valkyrie", likewise has its origin in the mythological location [[Valhalla]].

== Design ==
In February 2017, Aston Martin revealed most of the vehicle's specifications.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Padeanu |first=Adrian |date=15 February 2017 |title=Aston Martin AM-RB 001 will rock a Cosworth 6.5-litre V12 engine |url=http://ca.motor1.com/news/136137/aston-martin-hypercar-cosworth-engine/amp/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170216132237/http://ca.motor1.com/news/136137/aston-martin-hypercar-cosworth-engine/amp/ |archive-date=16 February 2017 |access-date=15 February 2017 |website=Motor1.com}}</ref> The final specifications were revealed later in the year. Several manufacturers, other than Aston Martin and Red Bull Racing, took part in the Valkyrie's construction, those being [[Cosworth]], [[Ricardo plc|Ricardo]], [[Rimac Automobili]], [[Multimatic]], Alcon, Integral Powertrain Ltd, [[Robert Bosch GmbH|Bosch]], Surface Transforms, [[Wipac]], HPL Prototypes, 3T Additive Manufacturing Polymers Ltd. (Prototal UK), Martins Rubber Company and [[Michelin]].

=== Exterior features ===
[[File:Aston Martin Valkyrie GIMS 2024 1X7A2266.jpg|left|thumb|The Valkyrie on display at the 2024 [[Geneva International Motor Show]].]]
[[File:2023 Aston Martin Valkyrie 11.jpg|left|thumb|Rear view of the Valkyrie.]]
A show car was initially unveiled to the public in order to give the public an idea of its design. The design was in a near-production-ready form.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Sheehan |first=Sam |date=14 June 2017 |title=Aston Martin Valkyrie revealed in most production-ready form yet |url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motor-shows-geneva-motor-show/aston-martin-valkyrie-specs-details-pricing |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170622204722/https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motor-shows-geneva-motor-show/aston-martin-valkyrie-specs-details-pricing |archive-date=22 June 2017 |access-date=15 June 2017 |magazine=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]]}}</ref>

The exterior of the car is extremely [[Aerodynamics|aerodynamic]] for a [[sports car]], with an extensively open underfloor that works on the [[Bernoulli effect|Bernoulli]] and [[Venturi effect|Venturi]] principles for purposes of [[Ground effect (cars)|ground effect]], and is capable of producing {{cvt|4000|lbf|kN kgf|order=out}} of [[downforce]] at high speed. Gaps on top of the car (for example, above the front axle and the roof intake) and a large front splitter aid in generating downforce.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Anderson |first=Brad |date=8 March 2017 |title=New Aston Martin Valkyrie Scares Off All Other Hypercars In Geneva |url=http://www.carscoops.com/2017/03/new-aston-martin-valkyrie-scares-off.html |access-date=11 March 2017 |work=Carscoops}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Perkins |first=Chris |date=14 June 2017 |title=Hey! The Aston Martin Valkyrie Has Headlights Now |url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/future-cars/a10016482/aston-martin-valkyrie-monaco-preview |access-date=14 June 2017 |magazine=[[Road & Track]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Westbrook |first=Justin T. |date=11 July 2017 |title=Aston Martin Has Updated The Valkyrie To Be Even More Unbelievable |url=http://jalopnik.com/aston-martin-has-updated-the-valkyrie-to-be-even-more-u-1796830134/amp |access-date=12 July 2017 |website=[[Jalopnik]]}}</ref> The wheels are also designed to manage the airflow and be as light as possible at the same time.  

Red Bull Advanced Technologies was responsible for the aerodynamic profile of the car, while Aston Martin, under the direction of Miles Nurnberger, oversaw much of the exterior design. According to Adrian Newey, “the green painted surfaces of that model are effectively Aston… while the black surfaces of canopy, rear wing and everything below the waistline are ours.”<ref>{{cite book |last=Newey |first=Adrian |title=How to Build a Car |publisher=HarperCollins |year=2017 |chapter=Epilogue |quote=the green painted surfaces of that model are effectively Aston under the direction of Miles Nurnberger, while the black surfaces of canopy, rear wing and everything below the waistline are ours}}</ref>

=== Interior features ===
[[File:Aston Martin Valkyrie Verification Prototype 001 Genf 2019 1Y7A5963.jpg|thumb|The interior of the near-production-ready Valkyrie VP-2 prototype.]]
The interior design was leaked online on 20 June 2017 and gave a preview of the car's design. The interior has no gauge cluster, but rather a collection of screens. By the left and right corners are the screens for the camera side mirrors. One screen sits at the top of the center console, which may have a collection of live vehicle information, and regular vehicle controls, but this is not confirmed. A screen is used on the race-inspired steering wheel and acts as the driver gauge cluster. Dials and switches sit beside the wheel screen to allow for easier changes without driving interruption. The seats, formed from hollow [[Carbon fiber reinforced polymer|carbon fibre]] straight into interior perimeter, are bucket variants, and have two seat belts for each car seat.<ref>{{cite magazine |date=20 June 2017 |title=Aston Martin Valkyrie's high-tech cabin leaked |url=https://theweek.com/hypercars/85838/aston-martin-valkyrie-within-sight-of-f1-speeds |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170620193939/http://www.theweek.co.uk/amp/hypercars/85838/aston-martin-valkyries-high-tech-cabin-leaked |archive-date=20 June 2017 |access-date=20 June 2017 |magazine=[[The Week]] |edition=UK}}</ref>

Because of the extremely small interior and doors (which are practically roof-only hatches), each seat is designed specifically for the owner's body shape through [[3D scanning]]. A removable steering wheel provides slightly more space for entry and exit.<ref>{{cite web |last=King |first=Alanis |date=24 May 2017 |title=The Aston Martin Valkyrie Won't Let You Get Fat |url=http://jalopnik.com/the-aston-martin-valkyrie-won-t-let-you-get-fat-1795508761/amp |access-date=13 June 2017 |website=Jalopnik}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Edelstein |first=Stephen |date=25 May 2017 |title=Aston Martin will 3D-scan drivers' bodies to make custom seats for the Valkyrie |url=https://www.digitaltrends.com/cars/aston-martin-valkyrie-3d-scan-seat/ |access-date=15 June 2017 |work=[[Digital Trends]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Saiidi |first=Uptin |date=23 May 2017 |title=Aston Martins upcoming car will 3-D scan your body for the driver seat |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2017/05/23/aston-martins-upcoming-car-will-3-d-scan-your-body-for-the-driver-seat.html |access-date=15 June 2017 |website=[[CNBC]]}}</ref>

=== Specifications ===
The car contains a 6.5-litre [[Naturally aspirated engine|naturally-aspirated]] [[V12 engine]] tailored by [[Cosworth]], which produces around {{convert|1,000|hp|kW PS|abbr=on|0|disp=flip}} at 10,500 rpm, with a redline of 11,100 rpm. This will make it the most powerful naturally-aspirated engine ever to be fitted to a production road car. With a [[Kinetic energy recovery system|KERS]]-style boost system akin to those fitted to F1 cars, the Valkyrie's hybrid system was developed by two main technical partners; Integral Powertrain Ltd, who supplied the bespoke electric motor, and Rimac for the lightweight hybrid battery system.<ref>{{Cite web |title=ASTON MARTIN VALKYRIE - THE ULTIMATE HYBRID POWERTRAIN FOR THE ULTIMATE HYPERCAR |url=https://media.astonmartin.com/aston-martin-valkyrie-the-ultimate-hybrid-powertrain-for-the-ultimate-hypercar/ |website=media.astonmartin.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=1 March 2019 |title=Aston Martin Valkyrie - The Ultimate Hybrid Powertrain for the Ultimate Hypercar |url=https://integralp.com/communication/aston-martin-valkyrie-the-ultimate-hybrid-powertrain-for-the-ultimate-hypercar/}}</ref> As a result, the full hybrid system contributes an additional {{convert|160|bhp|kW PS|disp=flip|abbr=on}} of power and a further 280 N⋅m of available torque with the certified max power output of the Valkyrie at {{convert|1,160|hp|kW PS|abbr=on|disp=flip|0}} @ 10,500rpm. Equally, with the full hybrid system, peak torque stands at 900 N⋅m (664&nbsp;lbf⋅ft) at 6,000 rpm.

At the same time the power output figures were released, the weight was announced to be {{convert|1030|kg|lbs|abbr=on|0}}, surpassing the intended 1:1 [[power-to-weight ratio]] with {{convert|1,126|hp|kW PS|abbr=on|0|disp=flip}} per ton.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Wren |first=Wesley |date=23 June 2017 |title=Report: Aston Martin's Valkyrie will have 1,130 hp, weigh just over a ton |url=http://autoweek.com/article/supercars/report-aston-martins-valkyrie-will-have-1130-hp |access-date=23 June 2017 |magazine=[[Autoweek]]}}</ref> The car can accelerate to {{convert|62|mph|km/h|disp=flip|abbr=on}} from a standstill in a time of 2.6 seconds.<ref>{{cite web |date=13 December 2018 |title=2018 Aston Martin Valkyrie |url=https://www.topspeed.com/cars/aston-martin-valkyrie/ke5679.html |access-date=5 January 2019 |website=TopSpeed.com}}{{dead link|date=January 2025|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> The exhausts exit at the top of the car, near the engine, similar to those of [[Formula One car]]s and the [[Porsche 918 Spyder]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Aston Martin Valkyrie - Engine View |url=http://icdn-3.motor1.com/images/mgl/MnYxY/s4/aston-martin-valkyrie-in-geneva.jpg |access-date=9 March 2017 |website=Motor1.com}}</ref>

Bosch supplies the Valkyrie's [[Engine control unit|ECU]], [[traction control system]], and [[Electronic stability control|ESP]]. The braking system is provided by Alcon and Surface Transforms. The front and rear lights are manufactured by Wipac. The car has all-carbon fibre bodywork<ref>{{cite web |title=Otherworldly Performance |url=https://global.astonmartin.com/en-gb/models/aston-martin-valkyrie#section_Design |access-date=13 May 2018 |website=Aston Martin}}</ref> and is installed with a carbon fibre Monocell from manufacturer Multimatic.<ref>{{cite magazine |author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |date=9 February 2018 |title=Aston Martin Valkyrie: specs, price and full details |url=http://www.evo.co.uk/news/17980/aston-martin-valkyrie-specs-price-and-full-details |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180604141042/http://www.evo.co.uk/news/17980/aston-martin-valkyrie-specs-price-and-full-details |archive-date=4 June 2018 |access-date=13 May 2018 |magazine=Evo}}</ref> Michelin supplies the Valkyrie with the company's high-performance Sport Cup 2 tyres, having sizes of 265/35-ZR20 at the front and 325/30-ZR21 at the rear.<ref>{{cite web |date=7 March 2017 |title=Michelin chosen as official tyre supplier for Aston Martin Valkyrie hypercar |url=http://michelinmedia.com/pages/blog/detail/article/c/a594/ |access-date=8 March 2017 |website=Michelin Media}}</ref> The wheels are constructed out of lightweight [[magnesium alloy]] ({{convert|20|in|abbr=on}} front, {{convert|21|in|abbr=on}} rear) with race-spec centre-lock wheel nuts to reduce mass. The Valkyrie and the [[Red Bull Racing RB16]] had its first shakedown at [[Silverstone Circuit]], where drivers [[Max Verstappen]] and [[Alexander Albon]] drove the car around the track.

==Production==
Aston Martin CEO [[Andy Palmer]] announced a policy in a Twitter post on 4 July 2017, stating that if the owner were to "flip" the car (buy and sell quickly to make a profit), the owner would not be provided the opportunity to buy any further special edition models from Aston Martin. This policy is also used for [[Ford Motor Company|Ford]]'s new [[Ford GT|GT]]<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lee |first=Kristen |date=6 July 2017 |title=Flipping Your Aston Martin Valkyrie Will Make Aston Very Mad |url=http://jalopnik.com/flipping-your-aston-martin-valkyrie-will-make-aston-ver-1796679652 |access-date=12 July 2017 |work=Jalopnik}}</ref> and [[Mercedes-AMG]]'s [[Mercedes-AMG ONE|ONE]] sports cars.<ref>{{cite web |last=Padeanu |first=Adrian |date=2 August 2018 |title=Mercedes-AMG Won't Allow Project One Owners To Flip Their Cars |url=https://www.motor1.com/news/260939/mercedes-amg-project-one/ |access-date=12 November 2018 |website=Motor1.com}}</ref>

Production of the Valkyrie ran from November 2021 up to December 2024. Aston Martin produced 150 coupés, 85 Spiders, and 40 AMR Pro models for a total of 275 units.<ref name="motor1" /><ref name="autoevolution" /> 

Ten cars, fewer than planned, were delivered in the last quarter of 2021, causing the company to miss its profit target. The company said that this only affected timing, all production had been sold but not yet delivered.<ref name="sales2021">{{Cite web |last=Sweney |first=Mark |date=7 January 2022 |title=Aston Martin will miss profit target by £15m after Valkyrie delivery failings |url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jan/07/aston-martin-miss-profit-target-by-15m-valkyrie-delivery-failings |work=The Guardian}}</ref> The Valkyrie Spider model was twice oversubscribed.<ref name="sales2021" />

== Variants ==
